    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "I never had a particularly strong relationship with mathematics, even though I did a bit of vocational training. I found it logical but not especially engaging. I’m more drawn to art and literature, where there’s more room for interpretation and emotion. Math was something I had to learn for practical purposes, not something I deeply connected with.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Not in a big way. I don’t panic or overthink math problems. My level of anxiety is fairly low. I’ve had a few moments of frustration when something didn’t make sense, but usually I just take a break and come back to it later. I find that yoga helps me stay calm and focused when I'm working through anything tough.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Yes, I used a chatbot to help me understand basic algebra. I wasn’t sure how to factor quadratic equations, so I asked it. It provided a clear explanation and even showed me step-by-step solutions. It wasn’t perfect, and sometimes it made assumptions I hadn’t made, but overall it was a helpful resource.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "First, you want to get the equation into standard form: ax² + bx + c = 0. Then, you can use the quadratic formula: x equals negative b, plus or minus the square root of b squared minus four a c, all over two a. You plug in the coefficients and simplify. If the discriminant is positive, two real roots. If zero, one root. If negative, two complex roots.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "You take the derivative of the function, y’ = f'(x). Then, you set y’ equal to zero and solve for x. Those x-values are where the function might have a maximum, minimum, or point of inflection. You can use the second derivative test to classify the points – if f''(x) is positive, it’s a local minimum; negative, a local maximum.",
